{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjYkipWbajca7rA9EtuV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018xi68LPXMgzXRyCg1dqXeC","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start of line, after the clef and two sharps: eight groups of four 32nds. They run E5 D5 C#5 B4 | A4 G4 F#4 E4 | D4 E4 F#4 G4 | A4 B4 C#5 D5, and then the same 16 notes again. That is all of bar 47: two falling-and-rising scale runs, 32 thirty-seconds. The first note is E5, high on the staff. So the line does not carry the end of bar 46, whose run rises from D4 to D5.\n\nBarline. Next comes B4 C#5 D5 E5 and then F#5 G5 A5 B5, two beamed groups of 32nds rising to B5. Then two beamed 8ths, A4 and E5 (the trill mark is not clear). Then D5 as a quarter, an 8th rest, and F#4 as an 8th. This matches bar 48 exactly.\n\nBarline. The last thing on the line is one note with tremolo strokes, around G4. That is the copyist's shorthand for the repeated 16ths that open bar 49 (G4 ×4, F#4 ×4 ...). The line breaks partway through bar 49, and the next line finishes it.\n\nNothing contradicts the edition. Bars 47–49 are correct.","right":true,"insights":"The 32nd-note runs in bar 47 and the rising run in bar 48 are easy to recognise, so the start was easy to place. The hard part was the last symbol: a single tremolo note standing in for the 16 written 16ths of bar 49. The edition's text also doesn't say how bar 49 might be abbreviated. A crisper image at the right edge would have made the end easier to confirm, as would the edition marking bar 49 as a tremolo/abbreviated figure."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5884,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5884},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"in